<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Deliberately empty.

      Notarization requires the hardened runtime, and the hardened runtime is
      only worth having if we do not immediately punch holes in it. Lumeshot
      needs none of the usual exceptions:

        * Screen and audio capture go through ScreenCaptureKit, which is gated
          by the TCC Screen Recording grant, not by an entitlement.
        * The app is not sandboxed, so writing captures to ~/Pictures, reaching
          the network, and using the login Keychain all work unentitled.
        * Citadel, SwiftNIO and swift-crypto are statically linked, and libcurl
          resolves to Apple's /usr/lib/libcurl.4.dylib.

      Sparkle is the one dynamically linked dependency, so library validation
      does apply to it. It needs no exception HERE: a Developer ID signature
      gives the app and the framework the same Team ID, which is exactly what
      library validation asks for. Local builds are the ones that cannot satisfy
      it, and they use Resources/Lumeshot-dev.entitlements instead.

      If a future change needs an exception, add it here with a comment saying
      what required it, rather than reaching for disable-library-validation.
    -->
</dict>
</plist>
